From 075114cb19bf97be3f190594d54f09aac3cf718f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Russell Oliver <roliver8143@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 12:28:27 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Bluetooth: mgmt: add
 MGMT_OP_ADD_REMOTE_LE_LEGACY_OOB_DATA

LE legacy pairing can derive its Temporary Key from data exchanged out of
band: both sides use the same 128-bit TK as the key of the c1 confirm and
s1 STK generation functions (Core specification Vol 3, Part H, 2.2.3 and
2.2.4).  The kernel has no way to accept such a key.  tk_request() only
ever fills smp->tk with zero (Just Works) or a 32-bit passkey, so a peer
that mandates LE legacy OOB - for example the Microsoft Modern Keyboard
(model 1780), a NoInputNoOutput device that rejects any Pairing Request
without the OOB flag - cannot be paired at all.

Add Remote OOB Data cannot carry the key.  Its payload holds hash/rand
commitment pairs for BR/EDR (P-192) and LE Secure Connections (P-256),
never key material, and the two payload layouts it already has are
discriminated by length alone.  Overloading a third length onto it would
make a command that today only publishes commitments start accepting a
secret, with no way for userspace to tell whether a given kernel supports
it.  Use a dedicated command instead, which a caller can probe for in the
Read Management Supported Commands response.

This patch adds the command and its storage; the Security Manager side
follows in the next patches.

The store is per controller, keyed on address and address type, with
atomic replace: a lookup racing with an add sees either the old or the new
entry.  It is mutated under a dedicated spinlock and read under RCU, so
the Security Manager can look an entry up from its own context without
taking hdev->lock.  Entries hold raw key material, so they are wiped when
released, never returned to userspace, and never logged.  Entries are
dropped when the controller is powered down unless the caller asked for
Persistent.

Also add Remove Remote LE Legacy OOB Data, without which a Persistent
entry could never be deleted.

Signed-off-by: Russell Oliver <roliver8143@gmail.com>
