diff --git a/Documentation/networking/bluetooth-le-legacy-oob.rst b/Documentation/networking/bluetooth-le-legacy-oob.rst
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..685c29d12
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/networking/bluetooth-le-legacy-oob.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,111 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+=======================================
+Bluetooth LE legacy pairing OOB data
+=======================================
+
+LE legacy pairing can use a 128-bit Temporary Key (TK) that both sides
+obtained over an out of band (OOB) channel.  The TK is used as the key of
+the ``c1`` confirm function and of the ``s1`` STK generation function
+(Bluetooth Core specification, Vol 3, Part H, sections 2.2.3 and 2.2.4).
+
+Unlike LE Secure Connections OOB, which exchanges a commitment value and a
+random number, LE legacy OOB exchanges the key itself.  ``Add Remote OOB
+Data`` (``0x0021``) therefore cannot carry it: its payload holds only
+``hash``/``rand`` commitment pairs.  Two dedicated commands exist instead.
+
+Both commands require a trusted (``CAP_NET_ADMIN``) management socket and
+are per controller.
+
+Add Remote LE Legacy OOB Data Command
+=====================================
+
+::
+
+	Command Code:		0x005c
+	Controller Index:	<controller id>
+	Command Parameters:	Address (6 Octets)
+				Address_Type (1 Octet)
+				Flags (1 Octet)
+				TK (16 Octets)
+	Return Parameters:	Address (6 Octets)
+				Address_Type (1 Octet)
+
+Possible values for the ``Address_Type`` parameter:
+
+.. flat-table::
+   :widths: 1 4
+
+   * - 1
+     - LE Public
+
+   * - 2
+     - LE Random
+
+``Address`` must be the address the controller will connect to and that the
+peer uses in the pairing transaction, that is the identity address for a
+peer that does not use a resolvable private address.  Entries are not
+matched against resolved identity addresses.
+
+Possible values for the ``Flags`` parameter:
+
+.. flat-table::
+   :widths: 1 4
+
+   * - 0x01
+     - Persistent
+
+   * - 0x02
+     - Force Legacy
+
+Without ``Persistent`` the entry is dropped when the controller is powered
+down.  With it, the entry survives power cycles and is only removed by
+``Remove Remote LE Legacy OOB Data`` or by unregistering the controller.
+The kernel does not write the key to persistent storage in either case.
+
+``Force Legacy`` makes the kernel clear the Secure Connections bit of
+``AuthReq`` in the pairing request or response sent to this peer, so that
+legacy pairing (and hence the stored TK) is used even though the controller
+is Secure Connections capable.  Peers exist that mandate LE legacy OOB and
+reject a pairing request that offers Secure Connections.
+
+``TK`` is the 128-bit Temporary Key, in the same byte order as the ``Value``
+parameter of ``Load Long Term Keys``.  The key is write only: no command or
+event returns it, and it is never included in debug output.  An all zero key
+is rejected, since that is the key Just Works uses and accepting it would
+mark an unauthenticated bond as MITM protected.
+
+Adding an entry for an address that already has one replaces it atomically.
+
+This command generates a Command Complete event on success or a Command
+Status event on failure.
+
+Possible errors::
+
+	Invalid Parameters
+	Not Supported
+	Failed
+	Invalid Index
+
+Remove Remote LE Legacy OOB Data Command
+========================================
+
+::
+
+	Command Code:		0x005d
+	Controller Index:	<controller id>
+	Command Parameters:	Address (6 Octets)
+				Address_Type (1 Octet)
+	Return Parameters:	Address (6 Octets)
+				Address_Type (1 Octet)
+
+When the provided address is ``00:00:00:00:00:00`` then all stored entries
+of this controller are removed, regardless of the Persistent flag.
+
+This command generates a Command Complete event on success or a Command
+Status event on failure.
+
+Possible errors::
+
+	Invalid Parameters
+	Invalid Index
